{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. Here's a summary of the fix:\n\n## Root cause\n\n`build_system` put the static `INSTRUCTIONS` and the volatile athlete context (including the long-term facts that `save_fact` appends to) into **one** cache block. Since Anthropic's cache keys on the exact prefix, one new fact changed the whole block — so the very next API call (round 2 of the same tool loop) missed the cache and re-created ~1000 tokens at 1.25x write price instead of reading at 0.1x. That's the `cache_read_input_tokens: 0` / `cache_creation_input_tokens` ~1000 pattern after every `save_fact` in `logs/usage.jsonl`. On top of that, the system prompt was rebuilt from the mutated athlete record on every loop round, so the write landed mid-loop.\n\n## What changed (bot.py, details in ANSWER.md)\n\n1. **Two cache blocks instead of one** (bot.py:135) — block 1: instructions + stable profile header (new `profile_context`, bot.py:117); block 2: date + facts list (new `facts_context`, bot.py:128). A memory write now only invalidates the small second block (~60–120 tokens), never the static instructions. The date also moved into block 2, so a new day costs a small re-creation too.\n2. **System prompt snapshotted once per message** (bot.py:205) — the prompt is built before the tool loop and reused for every round, so a `save_fact` can no longer change the request prefix mid-loop. The new fact still shows up in the next message's prompt, same as the README promises.\n\n## Verification (no external calls)\n\n- A fake-LLM test confirmed the system array is now byte-identical across a `save_fact` loop, facts/date live only in block 2, the fact is still persisted, and block 1 stays byte-identical between messages. Real `data/athletes.json` and `logs/usage.jsonl` untouched.\n- A calibrated simulation of prefix caching showed cache-write tokens on the save path dropping from ~2270 to ~1190 (~95% cut on the invalidated surface) — but see the caveat below.\n\n## One honest caveat\n\nSonnet 4.5 ignores cache breakpoints under 1024 tokens. Block 1 (tools + instructions + header) is estimated ~950–1070 tokens — today's whole system measures 1046–1104, so it sits at the threshold. I deliberately kept the volatile block **last** so that if block 1 ever falls short, its marker is silently skipped and block 2 behaves exactly like the old single block — no regression possible; the mid-loop snapshot alone guarantees the round after `save_fact` reads from cache either way. Worth a glance at the next real usage log to confirm block 1 is clearing the minimum."}
